/** \mainpage Robotics Studio 1 - Alpine Ice Perception & Mission System

@tableofcontents

@section system_overview 1. System Overview – Diagram

The **Alpine Ice Perception & Mission System** is an autonomous multi-robot inspection and hazard verification framework developed for **Robotics Studio 1 (41068)** at the **University of Technology Sydney (UTS)** by **Vu (Minh)** and **Owen**.

In high-altitude alpine ski resorts, supercooled water droplets and freezing fog accumulate along overhead ski-lift cables, creating dense cylindrical ice sleeves. Excessive icing shifts the cable's center of mass, induces dangerous catenary sag, risks cable derailment from tower sheaves, and threatens gondola passenger safety.

To autonomously detect, localize, and verify these hazards across rugged alpine terrain, this project deploys a **heterogeneous dual-robot architecture** operating in **Ignition Gazebo Fortress** with **ROS 2 Humble**:
1. **Aerial Scout (Parrot UAV)**: Rapidly traverses high-altitude cable spans, continuously tracking hyperbolic cosine catenary geometry and isolating ice sleeve candidates using forward RGB-D perception (Option B).
2. **Ground Inspector (Clearpath Husky UGV)**: Navigates snowbound mountain slopes under Nav2 guidance, executes real-time obstacle detours around lethal rock gates, establishes safe visual inspection standoff stances, and performs multi-frame optical verification.
3. **Mission Control & Operator Interface**: Real-time PySide6 and PyVista 3D visualizer, providing live telemetry, streaming camera HUDs, human-in-the-loop approval gates, and persistent SQLite database logging.

The system architecture and information flow between robots, sensors, simulation environments, perception nodes, planners, user interfaces, and evaluation tools is illustrated below:

