@section technical_methods 3. Technical Methods

This section details the algorithmic formulations, mathematical models, software architecture, and parameter tuning developed for the Alpine Ice Perception & Mission System.

@subsection catenary_math 3.1 Catenary Sag and Analytical Cable Geometry

Overhead ski-lift cables do not follow linear paths; gravitational loading induces catenary sag described by hyperbolic cosine curves. In `ice_cable_geometry.py`, each cable span @f$ s \in \{\text{span\_01}, \text{span\_02}, \text{span\_03}\} @f$ between terminal towers @f$ (x_0, z_0) @f$ and @f$ (x_1, z_1) @f$ is analytically modelled as:

@f[
  z(x) = z_{\text{mid}} + a \left( \cosh\left( \frac{x - x_m}{a} \right) - 1 \right)
@f]

where:
- @f$ x_m = \frac{x_0 + x_1}{2} @f$ is the midpoint of the span along the ski-lift corridor axis (@f$ X @f$),
- @f$ a = \frac{T_0}{\mu g} @f$ represents the catenary parameter (ratio of horizontal cable tension @f$ T_0 @f$ to linear weight density @f$ \mu g @f$), calibrated in simulation to @f$ a \approx 320.0\,\text{m} @f$,
- @f$ z_{\text{mid}} @f$ is the sag apex elevation at midpoint @f$ x_m @f$.

The nominal cable corridor lies along @f$ y_{\text{cable}} = -29.25\,\text{m} @f$. With dual cable tracks spaced @f$ \Delta y = \pm 1.25\,\text{m} @f$, the left and right cables follow:

@f[
  \mathbf{P}_{\text{cable}}(x) = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y_{\text{cable}} \pm 1.25 \\ z(x) \end{bmatrix}
@f]

Given any 3D point @f$ \mathbf{P} = (x_p, y_p, z_p) @f$ returned by aerial RGB-D or ground lidar sensing, the perpendicular Euclidean distance @f$ r(\mathbf{P}) @f$ to the cable centerline is determined by:

@f[
  r(\mathbf{P}) = \min_{\text{span}, \pm} \sqrt{ (y_p - (y_{\text{cable}} \pm 1.25))^2 + (z_p - z(x_p))^2 }
@f]

This mathematical formulation enables exact radial swelling verification:
- **Bare Cable Baseline**: Nominal steel cable radius is @f$ r_{\text{bare}} = 0.17\,\text{m} @f$.
- **Ice Sleeve Envelope**: Dense accretions swell radially to @f$ r \in [0.22, 0.45]\,\text{m} @f$.
- **Detection Decision**: A point cluster confirms physical ice if its 75th percentile radial elevation satisfies:

@f[
  0.20\,\text{m} \le r_{75} \le 1.50\,\text{m} \quad \text{and} \quad \|\mathbf{P} - \mathbf{P}_{\text{cable}}\| \le 3.0\,\text{m}
@f]

The upper bound of @f$ 1.50\,\text{m} @f$ discards noisy ground returns, returning background mountain meshes, or large blue simulation markers.

@subsection unified_detour_sec 3.2 Kinematic-Aware Swept-Volume Detour & Dual-Threshold Costmap Sieve Fusion Algorithm

Operating a 100 kg Clearpath Husky UGV across rugged alpine snow terrain requires negotiating narrow passages bordered by lethal rock outcrops, dense pine trees, and uneven snow banks. Traditional mobile robot architectures separate **2D costmap motion planning** from **local vehicle steering kinematics**. In rugged mountain environments, this decoupled approach causes two catastrophic, symmetrical failure modes:

1. **Phantom Obstacle Freezes (Perceptual Over-Conservatism)**: Standard Nav2 planning packages treat repulsive inflation halos (@f$ 1 \le c \le 98 @f$ in ROS 2 `OccupancyGrid`, @f$ 1 \le c \le 252 @f$ in Nav2 `Costmap2D`) as hard geometric barriers. In narrow mountain corridors, overlapping inflation buffers from opposing rock faces produce false dead-ends, trapping the robot or causing it to reject valid inspection stances.
2. **Chassis Corner-Clipping Collisions (Kinematic Under-Conservatism)**: Point-mass grid planners treat waypoints as collision-free if their center coordinates land on navigable cells. However, Clearpath Husky possesses a rectangular footprint of length @f$ L = 0.99\,\text{m} @f$ and width @f$ W = 0.67\,\text{m} @f$. When executing an in-place zero-radius yaw pivot (@f$ \omega_z \ne 0, v_x = 0 @f$) to face a detour bypass or aim the camera at an overhead cable pin, the chassis corners sweep a circumscribed turning disk:
   @f[
     R_{\text{circum}} = \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{L^2 + W^2} = \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{0.99^2 + 0.67^2} \approx 0.60\,\text{m} \implies R_{\text{active}} \approx 0.71\,\text{m} \text{ (with bumper padding)}
   @f]
   If the robot arrives at an obstacle boundary within @f$ d < 1.30\,\text{m} @f$ and pivots immediately, its corners swing into adjacent rock meshes.

To resolve both challenges simultaneously, the **Kinematic-Aware Swept-Volume Detour & Dual-Threshold Costmap Sieve Fusion Algorithm** (`husky_detour.py` and `husky_pin_view.py`) unifies costmap cell classification, goal-biased corridor slicing, swept-volume collision detection, pre-turn reverse kinematics, and optical line-of-sight gating into a closed-loop 5-stage pipeline:

@dot
digraph DetourKinematicFusion {
  rankdir=TB;
  fontname="Helvetica";
  fontsize=10;
  node [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, shape=box, style="filled,rounded", penwidth=1.1];
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=8, penwidth=1.1];

  c_in [label="Nav2 Costmap2D & OccupancyGrid\nGlobal & Local 2D Buffers", fillcolor="#e7f5ff", color="#1864ab"];

  subgraph cluster_stage1 {
    label="Stage 1: Dual-Threshold Costmap Sieve";
    style="filled";
    fillcolor="#f8f9fa";
    color="#495057";

    s_sieve [label="Costmap Sieve Operator\nSeparate Wheels vs Optical Line-of-Sight\nLethal: [99..100] & [253..254]\nPermeable Halos: [1..98] & [1..252]", fillcolor="#fff3bf", color="#f08c00"];
  }

  subgraph cluster_stage2 {
    label="Stage 2: Transverse Corridor Slicing";
    style="filled";
    fillcolor="#f8f9fa";
    color="#495057";

    s_slice [label="Corridor Pass Generator\nSample Y_pass in [-22.0 .. 0.0] m\nScore J(y) = |y - y_goal| + 0.3|y - y_curr|\nPriority: Open Southern Corridor", fillcolor="#d3f9d8", color="#2b8a3e"];
    s_appr [label="Approach Path Validation\nDiscretize segment (ds = 0.25 m)\nVerify zero lethal cells on approach", fillcolor="#d3f9d8", color="#2b8a3e"];
    s_slice -> s_appr;
  }

  subgraph cluster_stage3 {
    label="Stage 3: Swept-Volume Envelope & Proximity Trigger";
    style="filled";
    fillcolor="#f8f9fa";
    color="#495057";

    s_check [label="Forward Clearance Check\nProbe r in {0.6, 0.9, 1.1, 1.3} m, phi in [-0.25, +0.25] rad\nCheck Sonar (r_sonar < 1.0 m)\nEvaluate Yaw Error |Delta_yaw| > 0.15 rad", fillcolor="#ffe3e3", color="#c92a2a"];
    s_dec [label="Obstacle within d < 1.3 m?", shape=diamond, fillcolor="#ffc9c9", color="#c92a2a"];
    s_check -> s_dec;
  }

  subgraph cluster_stage4 {
    label="Stage 4: Deterministic 3-Point Reverse-Before-Turn";
    style="filled";
    fillcolor="#f8f9fa";
    color="#495057";

    s_rear [label="Rearward Clearance Verification\nCheck theta_rear for r in {0.6, 0.8} m\nEnsure d_rear >= 0.70 m", fillcolor="#ffe8cc", color="#d9480f"];
    s_rev [label="Linear Reverse Backup\nv_x = -0.15 m/s, omega_z = 0.0 rad/s\nTimeout Delta t <= 1.8 s (Delta d ~ 0.3 - 0.5 m)", fillcolor="#ffe8cc", color="#d9480f"];
    s_pivot [label="Standoff Yaw Pivot\nv_x = 0.0, omega_z = clamp(1.8 Delta_yaw, -1.0, 1.0)\nCorners sweep in verified free space", fillcolor="#ffd8a8", color="#d9480f"];

    s_rear -> s_rev -> s_pivot;
  }

  subgraph cluster_stage5 {
    label="Stage 5: Near-Field Conical Optical Gating";
    style="filled";
    fillcolor="#f8f9fa";
    color="#495057";

    s_opt [label="Near-Sight Conical Ray-Cast\n5-Ray Cone: [-0.35, +0.35] rad, range = 2.5 m\nsight_clear() to cable pin\nVerify 100% optical transmission", fillcolor="#e7f5ff", color="#1864ab"];
    s_commit [label="Inspection Stance Committed\nBegin 5-Frame Consensus Confirmation", fillcolor="#d0ebff", color="#1971c2"];
    s_opt -> s_commit;
  }

  c_in -> s_sieve;
  s_sieve -> s_slice [label="Lethal cell on path"];
  s_appr -> s_check [label="Detour pose selected"];
  s_sieve -> s_check [label="Nominal crumb reachable"];
  s_dec -> s_rear [label="Yes (d < 1.3 m)"];
  s_dec -> s_pivot [label="No (d >= 1.3 m)"];
  s_pivot -> s_opt;
}
@enddot

### Stage 1: Dual-Representation Costmap Sieve & Perception Differentiation
To reconcile the discrepancy between ROS 2 standard `OccupancyGrid` messages (scaled @f$ [0, 100] @f$ with @f$ -1 @f$ unknown) and raw Nav2 `Costmap2D` buffers (scaled @f$ [0, 255] @f$), `husky_detour.py` applies a dual-space classification operator:

@f[
  \mathcal{S}_{\text{drive}}(c) = \begin{cases}
    \text{FREE} & \text{if } c = 0 \\
    \text{PERMEABLE\_HALO} & \text{if } (1 \le c \le 98) \lor (1 \le c \le 252) \\
    \text{LETHAL\_OBSTACLE} & \text{if } (99 \le c \le 100) \lor (253 \le c \le 254) \\
    \text{UNKNOWN} & \text{if } c \in \{-1, 255\}
  \end{cases}
@f]

Crucially, the fusion algorithm differentiates **wheel traversability** from **optical visibility**:
- **Wheel Traversability**: Soft repulsive inflation cells (`PERMEABLE_HALO`) are treated as permeable by high-level corridor planning; the low-level DWB local controller safely regulates wheel trajectories through them.
- **Optical Visibility**: Camera photons travel unimpeded through open air. Repulsive inflation halos are strictly transparent. Only physical obstacle meshes block light:
  @f[
    \mathcal{S}_{\text{optical}}(c) = \begin{cases}
      \text{BLOCKED} & \text{if } (99 \le c \le 100) \lor (253 \le c \le 254) \\
      \text{TRANSPARENT} & \text{otherwise} \quad (c \in [0, 98] \cup [1, 252] \cup \{-1, 255\})
    \end{cases}
  @f]

@code{.py}
def is_lethal(cost: int) -> bool:
    """Handles both ROS OccupancyGrid (0..100) and Nav2 Costmap2D (0..255)."""
    return (99 <= cost <= 100) or (253 <= cost <= 254)

def _camera_cell_state(cost: Optional[int]) -> str:
    """Only physical obstacle meshes block optical line of sight. Halos are transparent."""
    if cost is None or cost in (-1, 255):
        return 'unknown'
    c = int(cost)
    return 'blocked' if ((99 <= c <= 100) or (253 <= c <= 254)) else 'free'
@endcode

### Stage 2: Transverse Corridor Slicing with Goal-Biased Proximity Metric
When the straight nominal path between waypoints @f$ \mathbf{x}_0 \to \mathbf{x}_1 @f$ intercepts a cell where @f$ \mathcal{S}_{\text{drive}}(c) = \text{LETHAL\_OBSTACLE} @f$:
1. The blocked line is pruned.
2. Candidate parallel bypass lines are sampled across the transverse snow corridor:
   @f[
     Y_{\text{pass}} \in \{-22.0, -20.0, -18.0, -16.0, -14.0, -12.0, -10.0, -8.0, -5.5, \dots, 0.0\}\,\text{m}
   @f]
3. Candidate lines are sorted by an asymmetric goal-and-momentum scoring metric:
   @f[
     \mathcal{J}(y) = |y - y_{\text{goal}}| + \alpha |y - y_{\text{current}}| \quad (\alpha = 0.3)
   @f]
   This strongly biases Husky toward the **wide open southern snow corridor** (@f$ y \in [-22, -10]\,\text{m} @f$) rather than the congested northern rock gate passages (@f$ y \in [-8, -4]\,\text{m} @f$).
4. **Approach Validation**: Intermediate samples along the connecting segment from @f$ (x_{\text{curr}}, y_{\text{curr}}) @f$ to candidate pass @f$ (x, y) @f$ are verified at @f$ \Delta s = 0.25\,\text{m} @f$ via `_approach_blocked()`. Samples within the immediate robot footprint (@f$ d < 0.80\,\text{m} @f$) are skipped to allow a robot already touching an inflation edge to cleanly pull away.

### Stage 3: Kinematic Swept-Volume Envelope & Proximity Trigger
Upon reaching a detour waypoint or inspection stand, Husky must orient itself to face either the next corridor leg or the overhead cable target:
1. Target yaw error is computed: @f$ \Delta\theta = \text{wrap}(\theta_{\text{target}} - \theta_{\text{current}}) @f$. If @f$ |\Delta\theta| \le 0.15\,\text{rad} @f$, the chassis is already aligned and no rotation is performed.
2. If @f$ |\Delta\theta| > 0.15\,\text{rad} @f$, `_forward_obstacle_near()` probes the forward swept-volume envelope over angular cone @f$ \phi \in [\theta_{\text{current}} - 0.25, \theta_{\text{current}} + 0.25]\,\text{rad} @f$ across radial shells:
   @f[
     r \in \{0.6, 0.9, 1.1, d_{\text{front\_thresh}}\,\text{m}\} \quad (d_{\text{front\_thresh}} = 1.30\,\text{m})
   @f]
3. Simultaneously, onboard forward sonar range @f$ r_{\text{sonar}} @f$ is cross-checked (@f$ r_{\text{sonar}} < 1.0\,\text{m} @f$).
4. If an obstacle or high-cost cell falls within @f$ d_{\text{front\_thresh}} @f$, in-place rotation is strictly inhibited to prevent corner clipping.

### Stage 4: Deterministic 3-Point Reverse-Before-Turn (Envelope Decongestion Maneuver)
When forward obstacle proximity triggers:
1. **Rear Clearance Verification**: `_rear_obstacle_near()` probes the costmap along the reverse heading @f$ \theta_{\text{rear}} = \theta_{\text{current}} + \pi @f$ for radial distances @f$ r \in \{0.6, 0.8\}\,\text{m} @f$ to ensure @f$ d_{\text{rear}} \ge 0.70\,\text{m} @f$.
2. **Linear Reverse Actuation**: If rear space is clear, Husky engages a controlled linear reverse:
   @f[
     \mathbf{u}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} v_x(t) \\ \omega_z(t) \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -0.15\,\text{m/s} \\ 0.0\,\text{rad/s} \end{bmatrix}
   @f]
3. **Maneuver Termination Criteria**: The reverse motion terminates when any of three conditions is met:
   - Forward clearance expands beyond threshold: @f$ d_{\text{front}} \ge 1.30\,\text{m} @f$,
   - Rear clearance reaches minimum safety limit: @f$ d_{\text{rear}} \le 0.70\,\text{m} @f$,
   - Time budget expires: @f$ \Delta t_{\text{back}} \ge 1.8\,\text{s} @f$ (producing @f$ \approx 0.27 - 0.50\,\text{m} @f$ displacement).
4. **Standoff Yaw Pivot**: With rotational envelope clearance restored, Husky executes the in-place yaw pivot:
   @f[
     \omega_z = \text{clamp}\left( K_{\omega} \cdot \Delta\theta, -\omega_{\max}, \omega_{\max} \right) \quad (K_{\omega} = 1.8, \; \omega_{\max} = 1.0\,\text{rad/s}, \; \omega_{\min} = 0.40\,\text{rad/s})
   @f]
   Because the pivot center has been translated backward away from the obstacle face, the full rectangular swept envelope @f$ \mathcal{D}(\mathbf{p}, R_{\text{active}}) @f$ remains completely within pre-verified free space.

### Stage 5: Near-Field Conical Optical Gating & Stance Confirmation
Once the robot is aligned, two complementary optical line-of-sight checks guarantee valid visual inspection:
1. `sight_clear(stand_x, stand_y, pin_x, pin_y)` casts a ray from the camera lens to the overhead cable pin, verifying that no physical rock or tree crosses the sightline using the @f$ \mathcal{S}_{\text{optical}} @f$ transparency sieve.
2. `near_sight_clear(stand_x, stand_y, yaw)` casts a 5-ray conical beam covering @f$ [\text{yaw} - 0.35, \text{yaw} + 0.35]\,\text{rad} @f$ up to @f$ 2.50\,\text{m} @f$ in radial increments of @f$ 0.30\,\text{m} @f$.
3. Stance commitment is authorized only if both tests pass, ensuring that 5-frame ground consensus confirmation begins with an unoccluded optical path to the ice accretion.

@subsection camera_reprojection 3.3 Slope Compensation and Camera Sightline Projection

Husky's EKF (`robot_localization`) is configured in planar mode (`two_d_mode = true`) to prevent z-drift during flat snow driving. However, the `snow_forest` terrain features a continuous 4-degree ramp climbing from @f$ x = 20\,\text{m} @f$ to @f$ x = 80\,\text{m} @f$. On this slope:
- Standard 2D TF assumes the robot remains at @f$ z = 0 @f$ with zero pitch.
- When projecting a 3D ice pin @f$ \mathbf{P}_{\text{pin}} = (x_p, y_p, z_p) @f$ into camera pixel coordinates @f$ (u, v) @f$, the camera optical frame is drawn too low. The resulting 2D Region of Interest (ROI) lands above the actual cable, causing false negatives.

In `husky_ice_visual_confirm.py`, **3D Odometry Pose Correction** restores the true camera transformation:
1. The node subscribes directly to 3D odometry (`/husky1/odometry`), extracting true elevation @f$ z_{\text{odom}} @f$, roll @f$ \phi @f$, and pitch @f$ \theta @f$.
2. The pin coordinates are transformed into the pitch-compensated camera optical frame:
   @f[
     \mathbf{P}_{\text{opt}} = \mathbf{R}_{\text{cam\_opt}}^{-1} \left( \mathbf{R}_{\text{body}}^{-1} (\mathbf{P}_{\text{pin}} - \mathbf{P}_{\text{husky}}) - \mathbf{T}_{\text{cam}} \right)
   @f]
3. Standard pinhole projection yields true pixel coordinates:
   @f[
     u = c_x + f_x \frac{X_{\text{opt}}}{Z_{\text{opt}}}, \quad v = c_y + f_y \frac{Y_{\text{opt}}}{Z_{\text{opt}}}
   @f]

@subsection altitude_follower_sec 3.4 Terrain Altitude Follower Controller

In Ignition Gazebo, both robots utilize `VelocityControl` plugins. Because Nav2 outputs purely planar 2D velocity commands (@f$ v_x, v_y, \omega_z @f$ with @f$ v_z = 0 @f$), a robot commanded by Nav2 will maintain its initial spawn elevation (@f$ z \approx 0.4\,\text{m} @f$). As the terrain surface rises along the 4-degree ramp:

@f[
  z_{\text{terrain}}(x) = \begin{cases} 0.0 & \text{if } x < 20.0\,\text{m} \\ (x - 20.0) \cdot \tan(0.07) & \text{if } x \ge 20.0\,\text{m} \end{cases}
@f]

Without compensation, the robot would tunnel directly into the rising terrain mesh.

`terrain_altitude_follow.py` sits between Nav2 and the robot base:
1. Intercepts planar velocities on `/cmd_vel_nav`.
2. Measures current altitude @f$ z @f$ from 3D odometry.
3. Computes the required vertical climbing velocity using a proportional controller:
   @f[
     v_z = \text{clip}\left( K_p \cdot \left( (z_{\text{terrain}}(x + x_{\text{lookahead}}) + h_{\text{hover}}) - z \right), -v_{z,\max}, v_{z,\max} \right)
   @f]
4. Blends planar motion with @f$ v_z @f$ and publishes the composite twist to `/cmd_vel`.

@subsection parameters_table 3.5 Parameter Specification Matrix

The key parameters governing perception, navigation, safety, and mission control are summarized below:

| Subsystem | Parameter | Default Value | Tuned Range | Functional Justification |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Aerial Perception** | `hsv_h_min` / `hsv_h_max` | 80 / 125 | [75, 130] | Isolates pale-blue cyan ice spectrum; rejects green pine needles and yellow terrain. |
| **Aerial Perception** | `hsv_s_min` / `hsv_s_max` | 28 / 240 | [25, 245] | Lower saturation bound (28) rejects pure white snow surfaces. |
| **Aerial Perception** | `hsv_v_min` / `hsv_v_max` | 90 / 255 | [80, 255] | Lower value bound (90) eliminates dark bare steel cable and shadows. |
| **Aerial Perception** | `min_bulge_radius_m` | 0.20 m | [0.18, 0.25] m | Minimum radial surface distance required to verify ice accretion above 0.17 m bare cable. |
| **Aerial Perception** | `max_bulge_radius_m` | 1.50 m | [1.20, 1.80] m | Upper bound rejecting large blue background markers, terrain blobs, or sky noise. |
| **Aerial Perception** | `min_cluster_points` | 12 | [8, 25] | Minimum valid 3D depth samples required within color bounding box to confirm geometry. |
| **Ground Perception** | `frames_required` | 5 | [3, 7] | Consecutive video frames collected at standoff stance for consensus voting. |
| **Ground Perception** | `positives_required` | 4 | [3, 5] | Majority threshold (4/5) required to transition status to `CONFIRMED`. |
| **Ground Perception** | `roi_half_width_px` / `_height` | 100 / 75 px | [80, 120] px | Optical bounding box centered on projected 3D target coordinates. |
| **Motion & Detour** | `standoff_m` | 16.0 m | [14.0, 20.0] m | Horizontal ground offset from cable, placing 8m cable in center of camera 20° elevation. |
| **Motion & Detour** | `drive_step_m` | 4.0 m | [3.0, 5.0] m | Short step size keeping Nav2 goals within active 10x10 m local costmap and cleared corridor. |
| **Motion & Detour** | `PASS_MARGIN` | 1.5 m | [1.2, 1.8] m | Clearance margin around rock gate centers ensuring robot footprint clears lethal cells. |
| **Motion & Detour** | `reverse_turn_dist` | 0.5 m | [0.4, 0.7] m | Linear backup distance opening rotational clearance before pivot in tight passages. |
| **Safety & Sensor** | `max_ground_height` | 0.20 m | [0.15, 0.25] m | LiDAR ground filter height threshold; drops 4° snow slope while retaining low rocks. |
| **Safety & Sensor** | `bumper_hold_seconds` | 0.4 s | [0.2, 0.6] s | Virtual laser scan persistence in costmap following solid contact detection. |
| **Altitude Follower** | `kp_z` | 1.5 | [1.0, 2.0] | Proportional gain for terrain vertical climbing velocity controller. |

@subsection tech_limitations 3.6 Technical Limitations and Unresolved Issues

While the implemented architecture demonstrated robust performance across all evaluation scenarios, several technical limitations were identified:
1. **SLAM Feature Sparsity on Snow**: SLAM Toolbox relies heavily on 2D LiDAR laser scans. In open snowfields devoid of nearby trees or towers (@f$ x \in [0, 20]\,\text{m} @f$), laser scans encounter featureless flat ground. Scan-matching uncertainty increases, leading to slight odometry drift until rock clusters or towers come into view.
2. **LiDAR Specular Reflectance**: The current simulation approximates LiDAR physics using standard diffuse ray casting. In physical deployments, glaze ice and wet snow produce strong specular reflectance, causing beam deflection and dropouts at shallow incidence angles.
3. **Severe Fog Optical Attenuation**: Color-based HSV segmentation assumes ambient lighting conditions with clear transmission. Heavy freezing fog or blizzards would severely attenuate color contrast, requiring active illumination or long-wave infrared (thermal) camera integration.
4. **Monocular Standoff Baseline**: Ground verification relies on a single RGB camera projected via known cable geometry. While highly reliable when cable elevation is known, true multi-view stereo or ground-based 3D LiDAR would be required if cables suffer unmodelled structural breaks.

---

@section simulation_robots_sensors 4. Simulation, Robots and Sensors

@subsection sim_environment 4.1 Simulation Environment (snow_forest.sdf)

The system operates in a high-fidelity Gazebo Fortress environment (`snow_forest.sdf`) specifically constructed to replicate alpine ski resort conditions:

- **Terrain Mesh**: A 100 m x 50 m snowbound mountain slope featuring a flat staging valley (@f$ x \in [-15, 20]\,\text{m} @f$) transitioning into a 4-degree snow ramp (@f$ x \in [20, 80]\,\text{m} @f$).
- **Ski-Lift Infrastructure**: Three consecutive catenary cable spans (`span_01`, `span_02`, `span_03`) supported by steel lattice towers (`tower_1`, `tower_2`, `tower_3`, `tower_4`). Cables hang at @f$ y = -29.25\,\text{m} @f$ with elevations ranging from 7.5 m to 14.2 m above ground.
- **Lethal Rock Gates**: Five strategic rock gate clusters positioned across the transit corridor:
  - Gate 1: @f$ x = 22.0\,\text{m}, y = 1.0\,\text{m} @f$ (forces southern bypass)
  - Gate 2: @f$ x = 36.0\,\text{m}, y = -9.0\,\text{m} @f$
  - Gate 3: @f$ x = 46.0\,\text{m}, y = -8.5\,\text{m} @f$
  - Gate 4: @f$ x = 52.0\,\text{m}, y = -11.0\,\text{m} @f$
  - Gate 5: @f$ x = 56.0\,\text{m}, y = -0.8\,\text{m} @f$ (forces northern bypass)
- **Natural Obstacles**: Clustered alpine pine trees along corridor flanks (@f$ |y| > 15\,\text{m} @f$) and dynamic snow rabbits hopping across drive lanes.
- **Dynamic Ice Storage**: 8 preloaded ice sleeve models (`ice_block_01` to `ice_block_08`) parked underground at @f$ z = -50.0\,\text{m} @f$. `ice_scenario_manager.py` dynamically repositions them onto cable spans via Gazebo services at mission startup.

@subsection robot_platforms 4.2 Robot Platforms & Sensory Configuration

### Aerial Platform: Parrot UAV (`parrot.urdf.xacro`)
- **Kinematics**: Simulated quadcopter utilizing Ignition `VelocityControl` with full 3D translation (@f$ v_x, v_y, v_z, \omega_z @f$).
- **Forward RGB-D Camera**:
  - Resolution: 640 x 480 pixels @ 30 Hz.
  - Horizontal Field of View (HFOV): 120° (2.0944 rad).
  - Mounting: Pitched downward by 45° (0.7854 rad), positioned at @f$ [0.25, 0.0, 0.25]\,\text{m} @f$ on drone frame.
  - Depth Clip Range: 0.4 m to 10.0 m.
- **Downward 1D Sonar Altimeter**: Range 0.1 m to 15.0 m for ground clearance monitoring.

### Ground Platform: Clearpath Husky UGV (`husky.urdf.xacro`)
- **Kinematics**: 4-wheel skid-steer differential drive platform (mass ~100 kg, footprint 0.99 m x 0.67 m).
- **Forward RealSense RGB-D Camera**:
  - Resolution: 640 x 480 pixels @ 30 Hz.
  - Mounting: Pitched upward by 20° (0.349 rad) to focus on overhead cables from a 16 m standoff stance.
- **SICK 2D LiDAR**:
  - Scan Range: 25.0 m, 270° horizontal arc.
  - Mounting: Pitched upward by ~7° to balance forward obstacle detection with terrain slope handling.
- **Contact Bumpers**:
  - 5 physical contact collision volumes: main chassis bumper box plus 4 individual wheel contacts (`front_left`, `front_right`, `rear_left`, `rear_right`).
  - Monitored by `husky_bumper_stop.py` via Gazebo contact sensor plugins.

---

@section perception_pipeline 5. Perception Pipeline

The perception pipeline is structured into two complementary stages: **wide-area aerial candidate screening** and **targeted ground multi-frame verification**.

@dot
digraph PerceptionPipeline {
  rankdir=LR;
  fontname="Helvetica";
  fontsize=11;
  node [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, shape=box, style="filled,rounded", penwidth=1.1];
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=8, penwidth=1.1];

  subgraph cluster_aerial_stage {
    label="Stage 1: Aerial Candidate Screening";
    style="filled";
    fillcolor="#e8f5e9";
    color="#2e7d32";

    p_in_rgb [label="Color Camera Feed\n(640x480 RGB)", fillcolor="#c8e6c9", color="#2e7d32"];
    p_hsv [label="Ice Color Filter\nCyan Spectral Range\nSnow & Dark Cable Rejection", fillcolor="#a5d6a7", color="#1b5e20"];
    p_morph [label="Morphological Clean\nOpen (5x5) -> Close (5x5)\nArea >= 35 px", fillcolor="#a5d6a7", color="#1b5e20"];
    p_backproj [label="3D Depth Alignment\nBack-project Pixels\n(u,v,d) -> (X,Y,Z)", fillcolor="#a5d6a7", color="#1b5e20"];
    p_catenary [label="Catenary Clearance Check\nRadial Distance r\n0.20m <= r <= 1.50m", fillcolor="#81c784", color="#1b5e20"];
    p_pin [label="Ice Hazard Pin Identified\n(3D Coordinates in Map Frame)", fillcolor="#66bb6a", color="#1b5e20"];

    p_in_rgb -> p_hsv -> p_morph -> p_backproj -> p_catenary -> p_pin;
  }

  subgraph cluster_ground_stage {
    label="Stage 2: Ground Multi-Frame Hazards Identification and Verification";
    style="filled";
    fillcolor="#e7f5ff";
    color="#1864ab";

    g_req [label="Receive Inspection Request\nTarget Pin Coordinates", fillcolor="#d0ebff", color="#1864ab"];
    g_tilt [label="Chassis Slope Compensation\nCompensate 4° Ramp Pitch\nRoll/Pitch/Z update", fillcolor="#a5d8ff", color="#1971c2"];
    g_proj [label="Camera Projection\nProject Pin -> Pixel (u,v)\nExtract 200x150 Region", fillcolor="#a5d8ff", color="#1971c2"];
    g_vote [label="5-Frame Consensus Engine\nHSV Detection in ROI\nRequire >= 4 Positives", fillcolor="#74c0fc", color="#1864ab"];
    g_res [label="Hazard Status: CONFIRMED\nSave PNG Evidence Snapshot", fillcolor="#4dabf7", color="#0c8599"];

    g_req -> g_tilt -> g_proj -> g_vote -> g_res;
  }

  p_pin -> g_req [label="Target handoff", color="#d9480f", style="dashed"];
}
@enddot

### Perception Algorithms and Tuning:
1. **HSV Segmentation**: Isolates the characteristic pale-blue/cyan spectral signature of dense ice (`H: [80, 125]`). Snow rejection is enforced by requiring saturation @f$ S \ge 28 @f$, while bare steel cable and shadows are rejected by requiring value @f$ V \ge 90 @f$.
2. **Radial 3D Elevation Metric**: Depth image samples within the candidate color bounding box are backprojected to 3D. The radial distance @f$ r @f$ of each point to the analytic catenary cable centerline is computed. Detections are confirmed only if the 75th percentile surface radius satisfies @f$ 0.20\,\text{m} \le r_{75} \le 1.50\,\text{m} @f$, confirming physical ice accretion.
3. **Temporal Majority Voting**: Ground verification evaluates 5 consecutive camera frames. Detections with at least 4 positive frames transition to `CONFIRMED`.
4. **Diagnostic Image Archiving**: Every inspection logs timestamped diagnostic imagery via `husky_path_log.py`, capturing `T0X_under_determination.png` upon arrival and `T0X_verified.png` upon conclusion.

---

@section decision_making 6. Decision Making and Path Planning

Decision making coordinates target prioritization ("where to go next") with terrain-adaptive path planning ("how to get there").

@dot
digraph DecisionMakingFlow {
  rankdir=TB;
  fontname="Helvetica";
  fontsize=11;
  node [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, shape=box, style="filled,rounded", penwidth=1.1];
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=8, penwidth=1.1];

  start_state [label="Start Mission\nParrot patrols cableway", fillcolor="#e8f5e9", color="#2e7d32"];
  pin_rx [label="Aerial Candidate Detected\nTransmit 3D Pin Coordinates", fillcolor="#c8e6c9", color="#2e7d32"];
  queue_mgr [label="Hazard Priority Queue\n- Merge spatial duplicates (<3.0m)\n- Filter false positives (>=3 hits)\n- Sort nearest-first along corridor X", fillcolor="#ffe066", color="#d9480f"];
  stand_gen [label="Generate Standoff Pose\nStand: y = y_pin + 16.0m (in [-22, 2])\nAim yaw facing target pin", fillcolor="#ffec99", color="#e67700"];
  step_loop [label="4.0m Drive Step Generator\nWalk forward toward stand pose", fillcolor="#d0ebff", color="#1864ab"];
  check_block [label="Is Straight Step Blocked?\nCostmap check for lethal rocks", shape=diamond, fillcolor="#a5d8ff", color="#1971c2"];
  detour_plan [label="Execute Detour Planner\nSelect nearest free corridor pass line\nPrioritize southern snow corridor", fillcolor="#74c0fc", color="#1864ab"];
  nav2_exec [label="Autonomous Navigation\nExecute Trajectory to Pose", fillcolor="#d0ebff", color="#1864ab"];
  bumper_check [label="Bumper Contact Detected?", shape=diamond, fillcolor="#ffc9c9", color="#c92a2a"];
  bumper_recov [label="Safety Bumper Trigger\nInject Virtual Laser Obstacle\nTrigger Autonomous Backup Recovery", fillcolor="#ff8787", color="#c92a2a"];
  stand_reach [label="Husky Reaches Standoff Pose", fillcolor="#d0ebff", color="#1864ab"];
  align_check [label="Obstacle within 1.3m of turn?", shape=diamond, fillcolor="#a5d8ff", color="#1971c2"];
  rev_turn [label="Execute 3-Point Turn\n0.5m reverse backup, then pivot", fillcolor="#74c0fc", color="#1864ab"];
  gate_check [label="Operator Approval Gate\nManual Authorization / Hold", fillcolor="#d0bfff", color="#5f3dc4"];
  verify_exec [label="Execute Ground Verification\n5-frame visual consensus", fillcolor="#b197fc", color="#5f3dc4"];
  db_log [label="Write Record to SQLite\nSave verified evidence photos", fillcolor="#d0bfff", color="#5f3dc4"];

  start_state -> pin_rx -> queue_mgr -> stand_gen -> step_loop -> check_block;
  check_block -> detour_plan [label="Yes (Lethal)"];
  check_block -> nav2_exec [label="No (Free)"];
  detour_plan -> nav2_exec;
  nav2_exec -> bumper_check;
  bumper_check -> bumper_recov [label="Yes (Solid hit)"];
  bumper_recov -> nav2_exec [label="Recover"];
  bumper_check -> stand_reach [label="No (Arrived)"];
  stand_reach -> align_check;
  align_check -> rev_turn [label="Yes (<1.3m)"];
  align_check -> gate_check [label="No (Clear)"];
  rev_turn -> gate_check;
  gate_check -> verify_exec -> db_log;
}
@enddot

### Decision Logic Highlights:
- **Corridor Step Discretization**: Driving in 4.0 m discrete steps prevents Nav2 global planner truncation beyond the 10x10 m local costmap window.
- **Priority Queue Allocation**: Targets are sorted dynamically by longitudinal progress along @f$ X @f$, ensuring Husky never backtracks unnecessarily across the mountain slope.
- **Human-in-the-Loop Gate**: `mission_gate.py` prevents autonomous inspection of unverified locations until the operator explicitly authorizes execution via `/operator/approve`.

---

@section operator_interface 7. Operator Guidance Interface & Data Storage

The operator interface subsystem comprises Owen's **PySide6 + PyVista 3D Operator GUI** (`fde_gui/scripts/operator_gui.py`) and persistent **SQLite Mission Database** (`fde_gui/operator_interface/mission_database.py`):

1. **Interactive 3D Viewport (`map_3d_widget.py`)**: Loads `snow_forest.sdf` terrain, tower meshes, and cable models into a hardware-accelerated PyVista VTK viewport. Renders real-time robot pose markers, waypoint trails, and color-coded hazard spheres.
2. **Multi-Camera Operations Panel (`live_operations.py`)**: Displays live synchronized video streams from Parrot's forward camera and Husky's RealSense camera using low-latency `sensor_data` QoS.
3. **Mission Progress Tracking (`mission_progress.py`)**: Real-time state indicator tracking operational phases (`AERIAL_PATROL -> CANDIDATE_PINNED -> UGV_TRANSIT -> CONFIRMATION -> LOGGED`).
4. **SQLite Database Persistence**: Every detected hazard is committed with unique UUIDs, spatial coordinates, detection timestamps, confidence metrics, and links to saved PNG evidence files.

---

@section verification_testing 8. Verification and Unit Test Suite

The system includes **11 dedicated unit test suites** in `test/`, validating core algorithms deterministically without requiring an active Gazebo instance:

| Test File | Target Module | Scope of Verification |
|---|---|---|
| `test/test_husky_detour.py` | `husky_detour.py` | Costmap lethal thresholds, corridor pass line selection, 3-point reverse turns, and line-of-sight ray checks. |
| `test/test_husky_pin_view.py` | `husky_pin_view.py` | Standoff pose generation, 4m drive step discretization, and target queue sequencing. |
| `test/test_husky_bumper_stop.py` | `husky_bumper_stop.py` | Snow ground contact filtering vs solid impact detection and virtual laser scan geometry. |
| `test/test_husky_ice_visual_confirm.py` | `husky_ice_visual_confirm.py` | 5-frame temporal majority voting, HSV color bounding box logic, and ROI validation. |
| `test/test_husky_path_log.py` | `husky_path_log.py` | Formatted evidence filename generation and portable path resolution. |
| `test/test_husky_ice_priority.py` | `husky_ice_priority.py` | Target queue ranking and corridor proximity sorting. |
| `test/test_husky_pose_correction.py` | `husky_ice_visual_confirm.py` | 3D odometry pitch/roll compensation and slope-corrected camera projection. |
| `test/test_ice_rgbd_detector.py` | `ice_rgbd_detector.py` | HSV color segmentation, snow rejection, and 3D catenary radial elevation verification. |
| `test/test_ice_target_tracker.py` | `ice_target_tracker.py` | State machine transitions (`TRACKING -> READY -> ACTIVE -> CONFIRMED`) and spatial clustering. |
| `test/test_detection_viewer.py` | `detection_viewer_core.py` | OpenCV dual HUD letterbox layout, bounding box projection, and stale-frame warnings. |
| `test/test_scan_ground_filter_pitch.py` | `scan_ground_filter.py` | Slope pitch compensation and 4° snow ramp ground removal. |

### Running the Test Suite
```bash
cd ~/41068_ws/src/MinhandOwen
python3 -m pytest test/
```

### Launching System Missions
```bash
# 1. Full Adaptive Pin-View Mission (Parrot Pins -> Husky Detours & Confirms)
ros2 launch 41068_ignition_bringup ice_pin_view.launch.py block_count:=4 enable_console:=true enable_viewer:=true

# 2. Operator GUI Dashboard
ros2 launch 41068_ignition_bringup 41068_operator_ui.launch.py
```

